  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 1.2. Increased institutional capacity of workers&apos; organizations</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Six major trade union confederations in Indonesia developed and completed a joint position paper on skills development beginning this biennium based on previous years&#xBF;f work. Additional studies and consultations were conducted by this biennium end, in consultation and participation of the unions taking to account the impact of COVID 19 on employment and skills. The sectoral and national studies and their renewed joint positions have enabled the unions to propose critical and much needed policy changes at national and sectoral level in the recovery strategies of the Covid pandemic by the end of the biennium.   
Furthermore, within the garment sector, the trade unions in August 2020 trade unions signed a joint commitment with the employers&apos; association to support the recovery of Indonesia&apos;s export-oriented garment sector amid COVID-19 pandemic. This process also lead to a demonstrated strong commitment for supporting and designing policy processes for their members in the time of crisis, including the proposals for a wage subsidy-program. 
Furthermore, at the sectoral level, union federations have demonstrated strong policy influence on the national policy agenda for COVID-19 response, where the migrant union SBMI  carried out a participatory action research (PAR) to map strategies on integrated protection services to migrant workers and enhancing gender-responsive services to women migrant workers and their families. Complementarily, the domestic workers unions Tunas Mulia, Sapulidi, Merdeka, Paraikatte actively advocated the domestic workers protection bill deliberated in the parliament. The bill is now in the parliament&apos;s national legislation priority list as at the end of the biennium. Furthermore, the union network in the palm oil sector, JAPBUSI (that consist of 4 confederations and 9 federations) actively involved in a series of dialogue initiatives to proposals to recover better from the COVID-19 pandemic, including proposals promoting freedom of association ILO has supported six major trade union confederations namely KSPSI, KSBSI, KSPSI, KSPI, K-SARBUMUSI and KSPN in developing a trade union joint position for skills development addressing the current employment issues. The ILO further supplemented the work of the unions with assistance in coordination with the workers bureau for additional studies on the impact of Covid on employment and skills to support the strategy formulation of the trade unions. 
For the sectoral activities, with the support from European Union, the ILO has supported a migrant-based union named SBMI to undertake a participatory action research on promoting village-based integrated protection and gender-responsive services to migrant workers. With the support from ILO RBTC, the ILO has supported several domestic workers-based organizations in advocating the domestic workers bill as well as the ratification of ILO C189 in Indonesia. With the supoort from the United States, the ILO has supported union confederations and federations affiliated to JAPBUSI in promoting workers&apos; rights in Indonesia palm oil through strengthening social dialogue, labour inspection, OSH and some other capacity building including COVID-19 prevention in palm oil sector. With the support from Japan, the ILO has supported six major union confederations, media union federations and creative industry union federations to promote OSH at workplace during the time of COVID-19 pandemic. It targets 10,000 beneficiaries, with 200 OSH promoters originated from the above-mentioned union organizations.

With the support from UNMPTF, the ILO has supported to Alliance for Ending Violence and Harassment at Workplace to raise awareness on anti-violence and harassment as well as promote the ratification of ILO C190. Furthermore, with the support from SECO, the ILO has supported trade union confederations namely KSPSI-AGN, KSPSI-AITUC, KSPI, KSBSI, KSPN and K-SARBUMUSI in promoting gender equality training at workplace through training of</narrative>
   </description>
